# go-learning — Fullstack Go Reference Project

A small, **complete** example of a production-shaped Go web app you can clone and copy
patterns from. The example domain (per-user **notes**) is intentionally tiny so the
focus stays on the things that actually matter in real projects:

- **Feature-based (vertical slice) structure** that scales as you add features
- **Cookie-based JWT auth** done properly: short access token + rotating refresh token
  (with reuse detection), all in HTTP-only cookies
- **Two surfaces over one core**: a server-rendered **htmx** web UI *and* a JSON
  **API** (documented with OpenAPI/Swagger) — both call the same service layer
- **Embedding** (`//go:embed`) of migrations and static assets → a single binary
- A dedicated **testing track**: unit (mock DB), integration (real Postgres), e2e

## Stack

Echo v4 · templ · htmx + Alpine.js · sqlx + pgx · Postgres 17 · goose (embedded) ·
go-playground/validator · golang-jwt v5 · swaggo/Swagger UI · `log/slog`

## Quick start

```sh
cp .env.example .env          # defaults match docker-compose (Postgres on :5432)
make tools                    # install templ, swag, goose, air (one time)
make db-up                    # start Postgres 17 (docker)
make run                      # generate code, run migrations, serve on :8080
# or, for live reload while developing:
make dev                      # air: rebuilds + restarts on .go/.templ changes
```

Then open:

| URL | What |
|-----|------|
| http://localhost:8080/        | redirects to the notes UI (login required) |
| http://localhost:8080/login   | the htmx web app |
| http://localhost:8080/swagger | Swagger UI for the JSON API |
| http://localhost:8080/healthz | health check |

> `make run` regenerates templ + swagger first. Generated files (`*_templ.go`,
> `docs/swagger/`) are committed so a plain `go build ./...` also works after clone.

## Architecture

Code is grouped by **feature**, not by technical layer. Each feature folder owns its
entire stack and registers its own routes. Adding a feature = add one folder; deleting
one = delete one folder. Shared infrastructure lives under `internal/platform`.

```
cmd/web/                 entrypoint: load config, connect DB, migrate, serve
internal/
  app/                   wires everything (reused by main AND the e2e tests)
  platform/              shared infra — NOT features
    config/              env (+ .env) → typed Config
    database/            sqlx connect + goose embedded-migration runner
    httpserver/          Echo setup, global middleware, central error handler,
                         CSRF + rate-limit, /healthz, graceful shutdown
    render/              templ → Echo helpers; CSRF/redirect/error web helpers
    validate/            go-playground/validator as echo.Validator
    apperr/              transport-agnostic domain errors
    logging/             slog + request-ID middleware
  features/
    auth/                register/login/refresh/logout; exports RequireAuth
    note/                per-user note CRUD
  shared/layout/         base.templ HTML shell
web/static/              vendored htmx + Alpine + app.css (embedded)
migrations/              goose .sql files (embedded)
docs/                    this project's spec, task tracker, generated swagger
e2e/                     end-to-end tests
```

### Anatomy of a feature

Every feature follows the same shape:

```
features/note/
  model.go         domain types + request/response DTOs (with validate tags)
  repository.go    Repository INTERFACE + sqlx implementation
  service.go       business rules (ownership, pagination) — the single source of truth
  web_handler.go   HTML surface (htmx + templ)
  api_handler.go   JSON surface (OpenAPI-annotated)  ← same service as web_handler
  views.templ      templ components (colocated to use the feature's own types)
  module.go        Module: holds deps, Mount(group) registers all routes
```

The key idea: **the service is the single source of truth**; the two handlers are
interchangeable delivery mechanisms over it. The web UI and the JSON API can never
drift apart because they run the same code.

### How modules plug in

Every feature exposes one method:

```go
func (m *Module) Mount(root *echo.Group)   // registers its own routes
```

`internal/app/Build` constructs shared dependencies once, builds each feature module,
and mounts it. The **auth feature exports `RequireAuth` / `RequireAuthWeb`**, which the
note feature receives as injected middleware — the one deliberate cross-feature link,
made explicit rather than hidden.

## Authentication flow

```
register ─► bcrypt-hash password ─► store user
login    ─► verify password ─► issue ACCESS (JWT, ~15m) + REFRESH (JWT, ~7d)
            both set as HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookies
            refresh token's SHA-256 hash stored in refresh_tokens (never the token)
request  ─► RequireAuth verifies the access cookie, puts user id+email on context
refresh  ─► verify refresh cookie ─► check hash is active in DB
            ─► REVOKE old + issue new pair (rotation)
            ─► if a REVOKED token is presented → reuse! revoke the whole chain
logout   ─► revoke refresh token, clear both cookies
```

Why these choices:
- **HttpOnly cookies** keep tokens unreadable by JavaScript (mitigates XSS theft).
- **Storing only the hash** means a DB leak can't be used to forge sessions.
- **Rotation + reuse detection** limits the damage of a stolen refresh token.
- **CSRF middleware** protects the cookie-based web forms; the JSON API is exempt
  (it's for non-browser clients) and relies on `SameSite=Lax`.

## How to add a feature

1. `mkdir internal/features/<name>` and create `model.go`, `repository.go`,
   `service.go`, `module.go`, plus `api_handler.go` / `web_handler.go` / `views.templ`
   as needed (copy the `note` feature as a template).
2. Define `Repository` as an **interface** so the service is unit-testable.
3. If the feature needs auth, accept `echo.MiddlewareFunc` guards in `NewModule`.
4. Add a migration: `make migrate-create name=add_<name>`.
5. Construct + mount the module in `internal/app/Build`.
6. `make run`.

## Testing

Three styles, each teaching a different lesson. See `docs/superpowers/specs/` for the
rationale.

```sh
make test              # unit tests only — fast, no database
make test-integration  # repository tests against real Postgres (needs `make db-up`)
make test-e2e          # full app booted in-process, driven over HTTP
make test-all          # everything
```

- **Unit** (`internal/features/note/service_test.go`): the service is tested with a
  **hand-written mock repository** (a struct with function fields). No framework, no
  DB — the mock mechanism is fully visible. Tests ownership rules and pagination math.
- **Integration** (`note/repository_test.go`, build tag `integration`): the **real SQL**
  runs against a **real Postgres**, catching what a mock can't — constraints,
  `RETURNING`, NULLs, FK cascades, ordering.
- **E2E** (`e2e/`, build tag `e2e`): boots the **whole app** (`app.Build`) with
  `httptest`, then drives it with an `http.Client` + cookie jar — asserting HTTP-only
  cookies, refresh rotation, logout invalidation, and per-user data scoping.

